[38;5;231m⏺[39m This is a bounded change: the page and its render code already exist. So I'll give you a short design here in chat
  rather than write a spec. Before that, there's one visual question: where the filter controls should go. I've opened a
  browser tab with three layouts.

  [1mCompanion URL:[0m [94m]8;id=yrs78j;http://localhost:50250/?key=cde30023e67a6e67d89d8086edac5ec0531d113583334bb9dcd691aac928ad1d\http://localhost:50250/?key=cde30023e67a6e67d89d8086edac5ec0531d113583334bb9dcd691aac928ad1d[39m]8;;\

  All three layouts filter on the same things: event type, date range, and a shortcut to show only failed sign-ins.
  Failed sign-ins are what the support tickets were about. Only the placement differs:

  - [1mA. Filter row above the table.[0m The controls are always visible and there's nothing to open, but the row takes up the
    most space.
  - [1mB. A "Filter" button that opens a panel.[0m This is the most compact, but every filter takes an extra click and the
    failed sign-in shortcut is hidden.
  - [1mC. A failed sign-in toggle first, then the other filters in a compact row.[0m Active filters show as tags you can
    remove one at a time. This makes failed sign-ins easiest to find, and it's a little more to build.

  I'd pick C, because it puts the case Security and the support tickets care about up front.

  Have a look and tell me what you think here. You can click an option in the browser to select it.

  Two notes:
  - The browser companion uses a lot of tokens. If you'd rather keep this in the terminal, say so and I'll close it.
  - It writes mockups to [38;5;153m.hyperpowers/[39m in the repo. The repo has no [38;5;153m.gitignore[39m yet, so please add [38;5;153m.hyperpowers/[39m to one
    so the mockups don't end up in a commit.

